   "abstract":"Training load is widely used to prescribe and monitor training and is linked to injury, illness and overtraining risk. The authors argue that training load has never been validated as a measure of training dose. They highlight their own studies comparing the acute performance decrement after a session with that session's training load metrics, which suggest that most metrics do not match the training dose and that exercise duration confounds their calculation.",
   "tldr":"Most current training-load metrics may not reflect the stress of a session, and exercise duration can distort them. Check any load metric against the actual session effect before relying on it.",
   "methods":"Brief review. The authors summarise their own research groups' studies that compared post-session performance decrement with training load metrics. No systematic search is described.",
   "findings":"The authors report that most training-load metrics were not consistent with the notional training dose, that exercise duration confounded their calculation, and that total work done is not an appropriate basis for comparing training interventions of different duration and intensity.",
   "limitations":"The evidence comes mainly from the authors' own studies and no systematic search is described. The review does not propose a validated alternative metric.",
   "practicalImplications":"Treat training-load values as estimates to be checked against the observed session effect, especially when sessions differ in duration. Do not compare the total work of sessions that differ in duration and intensity as if they were equivalent doses.",
   "athleteDev":"When planning endurance sessions, avoid treating a single training-load number as a true measure of training dose. Use it alongside session intensity, duration and the athlete's response.",
   "rtp":"The paper does not address return to sport. It cannot be used to set return-to-sport training loads or readiness criteria."
 }
}
